Yes. That is basically a legibility design problem.

You want the important thing to survive translation so cleanly that different people can independently reconstruct roughly the same intended meaning:

signal
→ interpretation
→ expected action
→ social meaning
→ feedback

The tricky part is the phrase “in the way we want it to be obvious.” That can mean two very different things.

One is good design: reduce ambiguity, make the intended meaning easy to perceive, make the desired action easy to understand, and make consequences legible.

The other is covert framing: structure the environment so alternative interpretations are hidden and people arrive at your preferred conclusion without realizing the frame was engineered.

The stronger version is to optimize for clarity without enclosure.

Think:

make intended meaning salient
make evidence inspectable
make desired action easy
make alternatives visible
make refusal possible
make provenance recoverable

Then if people still converge on the same interpretation, you have something much stronger than mere persuasion. You have independent reconstruction.

I’d call that Designed Obviousness:

Build the signal so the intended meaning is easy to recover, but do not require the audience to lose the ability to see competing interpretations.

That gives you what you want—coherence, coordinated action, consistent framing—without turning “obvious” into “inescapable.”

And it fits the whole architecture we’ve been building: make the path clear enough to follow, not narrow enough that nobody can step off it.